<h1>About</h1>

<h2>What is Yomimi?</h2>
<p>
  Yomimi shows you what parts of a book were adapted into what parts of the show. It's meant to
  assist in reading the book and watching the show at the same time. The idea is you read a bit,
  then watch the corresponding episode, then read a bit more, and so on.
</p>

<h2>Can I contribute?</h2>
<p>
  Yes! Yomimi is fully <a href="https://github.com/kowasaur/yomimi" target="_blank">open source</a>,
  so feel free to contribute anything you want, whether that's data for a show or improvements to
  the codebase.
</p>

<h2>Why did you create Yomimi?</h2>
<p>
  I am currently learning Japanese. As part of this study, I read manga and watch anime in Japanese.
  For some works, I'll read the manga and watch the anime. This is useful for learning, due to the
  repetition, but it's also especially useful for anime that do not have good subtitles. I can read
  the manga, stopping to look up words I don't know, and then in theory when I watch the anime, I
  should be able to understand it without having to pause or looks things up. I think it's
  beneficial to watch the related episode as soon as I finish the relevant chapters so that the
  meaning and context is still fresh in my mind. I thought to create this website when I wanted to
  do this for おにいさまへ but couldn't find any information about what episodes adapted what
  chapters.
</p>

<h2>What does Yomimi mean?</h2>
<p>
  Yomimi is a word I made up combining 読み (yomi), which means "reading", and 見 (mi), which means
  "watching". The name reflects the purpose of the website that I explained above.
</p>
